PHASE 1 - MINIMUM VIABLE ASSISTANT
==================================

What it does
------------
Continuously captures audio, transcribes it locally into a rolling timestamped
transcript, and on a global hotkey sends roughly the last 60 seconds of text -
and nothing else - to the local LLM for an explanation, which is streamed to
the terminal and recorded in the session log. No audio, transcript or answer
ever leaves the machine.


Running it
----------
  util/init_local_models.sh          once, and after every reboot of the LLM
                                     server; it loads the model under the
                                     identifier "meeting-assistant"
  helper/build.sh                    once, and after editing the Swift helper
  bin/rvw                            start the assistant in a terminal
  bin/rvw --source system --listen   start it already capturing, system only

Hotkeys (Hammerspoon, see hammerspoon/rvw_hotkeys.lua):
  alt-cmd-R          start or stop capture
  ctrl-alt-cmd-R     capture and analyse continuously (every 120 s)
  alt-cmd-E          explain the last 60 seconds

The same commands work from a terminal, which is also how to script them:
  bin/rvwctl EXPLAIN 90
  bin/rvwctl STATUS
  bin/rvwctl QUIT

Tests:
  util/run_tests.sh


Design decisions
----------------
Audio capture is a separate Swift process (bin/audio_capture) writing mono
16 kHz float32 PCM to stdout. System audio uses a Core Audio process tap, which
observes playback without sitting in it: no virtual audio device, no change of
output device, headphones keep working. The microphone uses AVAudioEngine.

Microphone and system audio are captured as two independent processes and two
independent transcript streams, labelled "me" and "them". They are never mixed
into one PCM stream, so my own words stay separable.

Recognition is cut on silence rather than on fixed windows. Utterance sized
chunks are independent, so there is no overlap to deduplicate and no half words
at chunk boundaries. A segment whose loudest sample is quiet is discarded
without being recognised, because Whisper invents filler phrases such as
"Thank you." when it is handed near silence.

Commands (EXPLAIN, TOGGLE_CAPTURE, ...) go through a dispatcher fed by a unix
socket. Hammerspoon owns the hotkeys, so the assistant itself needs no
accessibility permission, and Phase 7 can point the same client at the
companion Mac by replacing the transport under the dispatcher.

Transcript records already carry stream, start and end timestamps and text, so
Phase 3 can persist them without changing any producer. Nothing is written to
disk in Phase 1 except the session log.

The LLM is reached over the OpenAI compatible endpoint, so the model can be
swapped with the RVW_LLM_MODEL environment variable. Reasoning tokens are kept
out of the printed answer.


Measured on the M4 Air, 32 GB
-----------------------------
Speech recognition   9.9 s of audio recognised in 1.0 s (whisper-large-v3-turbo,
                     MLX), so transcription keeps up comfortably.
Explanation          129 s for one 60 second window with Qwen3.6-35B-A3B 4-bit.
                     That is not usable live.

The cause is memory, not the code: the 20.4 GB model plus a browser, an editor
and the speech model pushed the machine into 16.8 GB of swap, and generation
fell to about 5 tokens/s. Most of the budget went on reasoning tokens, and
enable_thinking=false is ignored by this model in LM Studio.


Rough edges
-----------
- The microphone stream is still unverified end to end. The permission is denied
  to the terminal that launches the helper and macOS no longer prompts, so the
  helper correctly refuses to run. Fix it in System Settings, Privacy and
  Security, or with tccutil reset Microphone, then toggle capture again.
  System audio is verified.
- The system audio tap needs the audio recording permission on first use.
- Continuous analysis is a plain timer that re-runs EXPLAIN; with the current
  model on this machine it cannot keep up.
- Whisper checks Hugging Face for the model revision at start-up. Set
  HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1 to keep start-up entirely local.
- Language is fixed to English by default (RVW_WHISPER_LANGUAGE).


Recommended before Phase 2
--------------------------
1. Choose a smaller local model for the 32 GB M4 Air. This is the Phase 8
   decision arriving early: the assistant needs a model that leaves room for
   the browser, the conferencing application and the speech model. The 35B MoE
   remains the right choice for software analysis on the 96 GB M3.
2. Test against real speech and a real accent, not synthesized speech, and tune
   silence_rms_threshold and min_segment_peak_amplitude in src/rvw/config.py.
3. Confirm the microphone stream end to end once the permission is granted.
